‘Fuck!’ Hannah Growled, slamming her headset against the desk. Second. Second yet again. It had been a pattern all season, and it was driving her mad. Silverstone should have been Ferrari’s to win; the track theoretically worked best with their car, and Hannah was sure that after coming top of the timesheet in both qualifying sessions and a late-day sprint race win, the main race could have been Ferrari’s too. Instead Charles had gone off in the twenty-third lap for no clear reason from the data they had in the seconds before his SF-28 went into the wall as exiting Woodcote. Lewis had been primed for victory, though, when the British weather turned the clouds above Northamptonshire an inky grey, and rain fell in fat drops on the tarmac. His decades of experience had helped as he cut through the pack after his pitstop. The team wanted the win for him too; a whisper from Fred the week before had informed them that after five seasons for the Prancing Horse, they were losing their Knight. Hannah was surprised he’d lasted so long, not in the least because racing was a young man’s game and young Ollie Bearman had nipped at his heels these past two years, having grown increasingly impatient at fighting for single points in the midfield with Haas. She secretly thought he’d only clung on for so long to outlast his old rival, Fernando, who had only finally given up the dream of a thirty-third win with a bitterly disappointing Aston Martin and retired last season. Even to the last they were competing. Hannah can still remember that season in twenty-twenty-three when she’d had to factor in Fernando too, flashes of brilliance that had pushed them to the limit. A valiant roar, and a pathetic whimper that team had turned out to be.
​A final win at the circuit where Lewis had ascended to the position of God would have been a picture-perfect ending to a career marked by triumph and tragedy. Hannah, along with every person in the garage with her, had wanted it just as desperately too; But like so many things she’d dreamed of, it didn’t happen. The Mercedes of George Russell, his former team-mate and the reigning world champion, had hunted him down ruthlessly. Hamilton’s years of expertise in difficult conditions were no match for the beast of German Engineering that had wanted victory too.
Hannah stood under the podium, red rain jacket pulled around her tightly, hood up over her hair. The hoards in red around her cheered on for their man. Some were missing, gone to soothe the fraught ego of Charles. The Ferrari golden boy, once thought to be the foil of Max Verstappen, had started to tarnish these past few seasons. She wondered if it might be a natural consequence of parenting a toddler, though the child was adorable; she remembered the hair pulling, screaming, and unreasonable logic of her own children with a shudder. Hannah thought his issues were mental, the car able to perform with Lewis; his skill was brilliant, having been one of the few drivers able to dance with Max and not see the wall during her Red Bull years. But in classic Ferrari fashion, Charles seemed supernaturally gifted at snatching defeat from the jaws of victory. Despite her sadness for Charles, she still cheered for Lewis. Smiling inwardly at how in twenty-twenty-one he’d been the spectre haunting her season. She’d been desperate for him to be pulled from the running, have some act of God (or Steward) throw him out of contention to let Red Bull take the ultimate prize. Now her voice was hoarse from shouting him forward.
​
The music started through the speakers; she could feel the sound in her chest as ‘God Save The King’ played for George. Even tired from the race, he really did look glorious; the epitome of the romantic racing driver ideal. George mopped the tear that had rolled down his high cheekbone with the sleeve of his race suit, his mouth tight as he tried to push down his emotions. Hannah found it sweet as his lip quivered; she guessed achieving the boyhood dream of winning at his famous home race circuit was no match for the cool and calm nonchalance these men tried to portray when they’d reached champion status. George lost it completely, however, when the hand of his childhood hero patted his shoulder. Her focus on the sentimental scene above her doesn’t last, eyes moving to George’s race engineer. Hannah felt the air knocked from her chest, hand going to the golden band around her right ring finger. Laura.
She’d joined Mercedes in a move that had shocked the paddock, taking over from Marcus Dudley at the end of George’s second Championship win in twenty-eight. He’d had to step back due to personal reasons; Hannah had heard whispers ranging from an out-of-control coke habit, a sports-betting problem, and a marriage breakdown. Though she had yet to find the true cause of his step back away from trackside operations.
Her caramel hair, cut up to her shoulders, was damp. The rain now a mizzle that seemed to seep into everything. Hannah could see her eyes were damp too. The fifteenth woman on a Formula One podium; the German anthem for Mercedes may as well have been played only for her. Another piece of history now belonged to Laura. The sound of Les Toreadors cut through the chatter of the rabble; the podium sprang into life. George was an old hand at champagne celebrations now nine years into his career; he caught Laura off-guard as she lifted her bottle and drenched her temple. Her golden-brown hair was dark, sodden with wine. Laura had never in her life backed down from a challenge, and promptly gave chase to George. Hannah was sure she could hear the rapturous laughter as Laura hunted him down, seeking out her revenge. Sure she’d never seen Laura’s smile so wide until George slipped and hit the ground; the crowd jeered. Laura saw her moment and dumped what looked like half a bottle of champagne into George’s once coiffed hair.
Hannah felt a tear roll down her already dampened cheek, the British weather giving her cover as George and Laura stood on the top step together as Mercedes media took their picture. Her throat tightened as she looked upward to the woman she still loved, watching on as Laura got everything she’d ever dreamed of. Drenched in champagne and sweat, arms wrapped around each other in camaraderie, the two beamed. Hannah thought back to the night she met Laura in twenty-twenty-two; terrified of even asking her colleague not to squeeze her ass on a night out. If the anxious girl Hannah had met all those years ago in sweaty São Paulo knew of her destiny, Hannah is sure Laura wouldn't have believed her. So insecure, so petrified of failure, it froze her stuck. The Laura above her, a thirty-eight-year-old successful woman, was a world away; shoulders back, letting her smile consume her face. Like she knew she belonged, Hannah thought.
They’d both changed so much since that weekend. At that point Hannah had barely been able to see out of the cage her post-natal depression had locked her inside. She’d hated herself, shrunk away from the confidence she’d built up over the years. Hannah didn’t hate herself now. She’d flourished in Maranello; it had been hard work getting the respect of a team infamous for not letting women outside of their PR or Media department. But she’d managed it. Late nights, no complaining about having to pick up the slack well below her pay grade, and being wholly unafraid to make her voice the only one heard. They’d even managed a constructors championship last year. Eked out from Mercedes mistakes, and a disappointing run of form from Antonelli. That hadn’t mattered; the champagne still tasted the same on her tongue.
Hannah felt at peace with herself now too; no husband to cling around her ankles like a weight to drown her. He was in Zurich, married last summer too and expecting a much-wanted son. Unsurprisingly, with the junior intern he’d kissed during their marriage. Though Hannah hadn’t known it was her until an argument about him having to move yet another weekend with the girls. At least he wasn’t her problem now; Emma would soon learn the type of man he was. Hannah tried her best not to let their issues bleed onto the girls. They’d flourished too in Moderna, and Hannah wanted to keep them growing into inquisitive and confident young women. The type of person she’d waited far too long to become. She’d felt so much freer since moving to Italy; truly a horse able to stretch its legs and run. No matter the struggle at work to make her voice known, she was unencumbered to be her very truest self. Even her sexuality couldn’t drag her down anymore; the thought of who she truly was and had always been didn’t fill her with terror now. She’d even been dating a little, even if deep down, her heart still belonged to Laura. They hadn’t spoken much since that night in Milton Keynes, not properly. Hannah still longed to be in her arms again; the smile Laura would give her, their eyes lingering a fraction too long when their paths would cross in the paddock, still made her feel suffocatingly sad. The wound in her heart is no longer as painful, though.
