Hannah sat in the pub surrounded by her old Red Bull colleagues; even GP in his garish ‘papaya’ top had joined them. She had an early flight back to Bologna in the morning, but had been able to sneak away for the evening and spend it with old friends. The change in teams did little to reduce the companionship between old dogs who had fought their way across the world for glory over the past two decades. She loved her life in Italy; the food, the culture, the weather. It wouldn’t be forever, so she tried desperately to make the most of every moment; but Hannah did miss England terribly sometimes. She finished the dregs of her Guinness, wanting another before she turned in for the night, and slowly worked her way through the pack of tired paddock workers to the bar. Her hand rested on the dark wood; she needed to get her nails done when she got back, Hannah thought. Slightly embarrassed at the dark red gel polish that had started to chip terribly.
‘Hello.’ Hannah turned. For a moment it felt like they were the only two people in the world.
‘Hi.’ Quiet hung between them for a moment, awkwardly trying to figure out the boundaries between them now. Hannah was unsure whether she should stretch out her hand or hug her. Laura thankfully chose for her, pulling her tightly into a hug. Hannah could smell the coconut shampoo she still used, no doubt having had to scrub at the champagne George had sprayed into her hair. Grateful for even a small moment with her.
‘I’m so proud of you,’ Hannah murmured, her breath warm and tickly against Laura’s ear.
‘I saw you,’ Laura admitted. ‘I’m glad you were there.’
‘I’m glad I was there, too.’
Laura moved back slightly, her hands still gripped at Hannah’s arm.
‘It’s mental…Felt so quick too!’ Laura grinned, eyes dreamy.
‘You’ll enjoy it more next time, I promise.’
‘Spoken like an expert,’ Laura teased with a roll of her eyes.
‘Well, I have been up there three times.’
‘Not bragging or anything…’ Laura laughed, squeezing Hannah’s arm.
‘I mean it. You enjoy it more the second time around. You’d better be more prepared to get George then.’
‘I got him pretty well.’
‘He might not fall over like a pillock next time…You need to be prepared’
Laura pulled back, ‘Why don’t we go outside, have a proper catch-up? –’ Her words drowned out as the bar erupted in more cheering as what Hannah thought was Arsenal scored, the large, glowing TV screen the focus of attention now.
Hannah nodded. ‘Sounds good to me.’
Beers ordered and now in hand, Hannah followed her down past the toilets and out into the garden; the constant mizzle meant apart from the smokers needing a nicotine fix, they were alone. Hannah was ashamed at enjoying the view of Laura’s bum as she trailed behind her.
‘You look well…Really well,’ Laura smiled. Hannah thought it didn’t quite reach her eyes, but that might have been wishful thinking on her end. A sign that Laura had been struggling in the eighteen or so months since they’d finally called it quits. ‘I think Italy agrees with you.’
Hannah nodded, taking a sip of her Guinness. ‘I think so too…Rains the same as Milton Keynes though, so I don’t quite get it all.’
‘I knew you’d like it.’ Laura gloated gently.
‘Because you know everything?’
‘I know you.’ Laura insisted
‘I still don’t like that,’ Hannah hummed. Even now it felt like Laura could see beyond the pleasantries and into her soul.
‘You got used to it.’​
‘I miss it…I didn’t like it– I don't like it. But I miss it.’’ Hannah said tentatively. ‘I miss you.’
‘I miss you too.’ Laura said, her gaze looking away from Hannah’s. ‘How are the girls?’ Laura asked, trying rapidly to change the subject.
‘Good, really good actually.’ Hannah beamed, fumbling in her pocket for her phone. Showing Laura a picture of them standing in front of Schumacher’s F2004.’
‘Good choice.’ Laura smirked. ‘They look happy…Have they stopped pulling each other’s hair out?’
‘Mostly.’ Hannah hummed, tucking her phone away.
‘Thank god for that.’
‘You’re telling me… They get a new baby brother in October, and I would not want to get that phone call,’ Hannah shuddered.
‘Oh fuck,’ Laura’s eyes went wide. ‘How do you feel?’
‘Relief that it’s not me mainly.’ Hannah sighed. ‘Not much…Wish he’d not flake on the girls as much, but what can you do?’
‘I have some suggestions,’ Laura bristled. ‘Fucking hate him.’
Hannah smirked. Even now, Laura still held a grudge.
‘You just need a sexy Italian on your arm,’ Laura schemed, ‘A Monica Bellucci type.’ She added, sipping her beer. ‘...Have you been seeing anyone?’ Laura asked, her voice nonchalant but her jaw tight.
‘I’ve dated a bit… You know how it is, we have big jobs that seem to take up everything else.’ Hannah muttered. Her eyes found the twinkle on Laura’s finger.
‘Though you don’t seem to be struggling.’ The ring was large, the diamond stone in the centre surrounded by smaller diamonds as icy as the platinum band it was set into. It clashed terribly with the gold of her other jewellery and looked wholly unlike what Laura would have picked out for herself. Hannah thinks it looked more like someone staking a claim on her rather than a promise of a life together; letting the whole world know Laura was a prize for them to gloat that they’d won.
Laura’s cheeks flushed with embarrassment as she tucked her hand under the table. ‘I was going to tell you.’
‘You don’t need to,’ Hannah smiled, though Laura knew it wasn’t true. She’d seen enough of Hannah’s mask to know the real side of her. ‘We aren’t together…I’m happy for you.’
‘Thanks,’ Laura muttered as she took greedy sips of her beer. Wanting to anaesthetise the shame welling in her chest.
‘Are you Happy–’ Hannah asked. Both genuinely hopeful Laura was, but aware she had never wanted a traditional path for herself in the years they’d known each other.
‘Yes,’ Laura snapped.
Hannah doesn’t challenge her. ‘Then that’s all that matters,’ she reassured. ‘So… what’s he like?’
‘Works in the aero department at Merc… we like the same music, like the same beer…Want the same things… I guess.’
‘That's nice.’ Hannah smiled. Feeling her stomach drop. She saw herself in Laura, and she wanted to cry. Even now, successful and accomplished Laura was still cursed with a need to be wanted. Remembering how readily she’d wandered into a marriage she had told herself she wanted, all because it was what she had thought she wanted; what she’d been told to want. Hannah could see Laura walking towards the same fate she had, and she despised it. Even more, Hannah felt protective of the part of her that had made such a devastating mistake; if she could save Laura, it could almost be like saving herself.  Laura had truly blossomed now; her career set to explode even more if George got his third championship this year with her on the radio. Hannah could shake her, yell that she shouldn’t sacrifice it for anyone. She instead chose tact.
‘But you don’t have to–’ she tried to delicately remind her, but Laura cut her off.
‘I don’t want to be alone, Hannah. He knows the world we’re in… He’s stable.’
‘I understand,’ Hannah conceded. She knew very well about clinging to someone who wasn’t right to be alone. Having a husband also seemed to be another layer of credibility in their backwards world. Expected to do it all happily while being fuckable and happy, and never let their smiles fall. ‘Am I invited to your wedding then?’ Hannah teased, trying to break the tension that had fallen between them.
‘Absolutely fucking not.’ Laura snorted.
‘I’m wounded.’ Hannah mocked, hand going to her chest and feigning a look of sadness. ‘You’re the one who said we should be friends…’
‘Is that really a smart idea?’ Laura asked, eyes locked onto Hannah’s. Laura knew she’d bend to Hannah even now. It didn’t matter how many years had passed, or whose ring was on her finger. She was still utterly Hannah’s. She felt too old to be starting again, and Laura had grown weary of returning to her empty flat in Banbury; now rented out for a larger house in the Brackley suburbs with Tom. He was kind enough, and had seemed to get over his disappointment at her firmness about not having a baby. The reminders that her clock was running out had made things tense for a few months last year. Laura certainly didn’t want more of the tidal push and pull that had swallowed a large chunk of her thirties; Hannah flitted between rage and desire back then. Maybe it would be different now, she thought. No. Absolutely not, she scolded herself.
‘When have we ever made smart decisions with each other?’ Hannah smiled sadly.
‘We wasted so much time…’ Laura chewed at her cheek, still haunted by what had happened between them. She still sometimes dreamed of walking down Hannah’s steps that evening in January twenty-eight, unable to leave fully until she woke up feeling trapped, and completely drenched in sweat.
‘I wish I could go back… I’d do it all differently.’ Hannah’s voice cracked, though she tried to cover it with a cough. Laura watched as she drained the rest of her pint.
‘You’ll have a hangover if you aren’t careful.’ Trying to avoid the sadness, she also ached for things to have been different.
‘Just wait until you hit forty…They get even worse,’ Hannah groaned. Laura was now a year older than she’d been when they started seeing each other. It still felt like yesterday; even now she would still find herself reaching for her phone to message Laura on impulse, hit with bitter disappointment when her brain would catch up, and she’d remember that door had closed.
‘Don’t remind me,’ Laura groaned.
‘Why? Forty was the best decade of my life? Plus you haven’t had kids, your boobs aren’t tucked in your belt yet.’ Hannah teased.
‘They’re not far off,’ Laura groaned.
‘Shut up,’ Hannah laughed. ‘I was always so jealous,’ she admitted, making Laura blush.
‘And my hairdresser keeps finding more greys.’
‘Why do you think I got more highlights?’ Hannah said, dipping her head forward, her greys twinkling in the setting sun.
‘Yeah, but my forehead is worse,’ Laura counted, wrinkling her eyebrows and making Hannah giggle at the stupid look on her face. Hannah thought Laura was growing even more beautiful with age. Admittedly, maybe she just thought Laura was, and had always been, beautiful. ​
‘Forty didn’t stop me… that’s all I’m saying.’ Hannah shrugged.
‘Just the hangovers… and the menopause–’
‘And the idiot husband.’ Laura’s smile dropped when Hannah mentioned ‘husband’. Hannah pretended not to notice.
‘I’m starving,’ Hannah yawned, stretching her arms upward. She felt stiff from being hunched over her desk, and then the pit wall all afternoon. ‘Up for dinner?’
Laura felt a pang in her chest. It had never just been dinner with them, remembering a time in a shitty Italian in Brackley she still avoided with Tom that had just meant to be food but ended up with them fucking in Hannah’s car.
‘Are you sure that’s wise?’
‘Well, you warned me about the hangover; I want some carbs to mop the beer up…Also I missed lunch.’ Hannah shrugged. Her voice breezy,
‘Don’t be coy with me, I know you, remember?’
‘It’s just dinner, Laura.’
Laura looked into her grey eyes and felt hunger. ‘I could eat.’
